decisions:
  - id: d1
    decision: Bundle A (lore type removal) shipped as a standalone commit (622cdaeb) BEFORE bundles B+C+D+E so a dedicated reviewer pass could validate the most lore-critical change in isolation
    rationale: User explicitly flagged lore as paramount infrastructure; narrow-scope-with-dedicated-review per the lore-criticality memory. The reviewer caught the F-1 assessment back-door; if Bundle A had been bundled with the others, that defect would have shipped to main.
  - id: d2
    decision: Storage-layer recordLore() rejection at write boundary, not just MCP handler rejection
    rationale: Bundle A's MCP rejection only defended the MCP surface. Builder flagged 3 CLI back-doors during Bundle A review. Storage-layer rejection defends ALL callers (CLI, MCP, future) uniformly — single point of enforcement is the right architecture for lore-critical code.
  - id: d3
    decision: Migration shim adds `v6-migrated:from-decision` tag instead of silent remap
    rationale: 'Jinx F-2 finding: lore is paramount; silent transformation of user data violates that principle. Tag provides forensic recovery path — users can find their pre-v6 decisions later via paradigm_lore_search.'
  - id: d4
    decision: PLSAT regression harness shifted to graceful-skip mode rather than removed
    rationale: Forward-compatible. Downstream adopters running the harness on a clean v6 install get the structural assertions (manifest validation, schema integrity, server route smoke). Source-vs-pack equivalence assertions gracefully no-op when sources absent. Harness becomes permanent regression infrastructure, not a one-shot v6.0 hack.
  - id: d5
    decision: loadPortalConfigLegacy = clean delete, not throw-stub
    rationale: Security audit verified the symbol was never on the public npm surface (no exports map). Throw-stub would imply a public API contract that never existed. Clean delete is honest and correct; security cleared Jinx's stub-fallback concern.
  - id: d6
    decision: Wisdom type='decision' stays soft this release
    rationale: D3 locked + unanimous team. Lore was the three-way-fracture offender that needed hard removal. Wisdom was the documented ADR path that earned a longer grace period. Symmetry-for-its-own-sake undoes the asymmetric justification.
learnings:
  - 'Lore-critical work pattern: narrow-scope-with-dedicated-reviewer (Bundle A alone before chaining) catches defects that bundled-pass-through would miss. Bundle A''s reviewer pass surfaced the F-1 assessment back-door that would have shipped to main otherwise.'
  - Single-point-of-enforcement at storage layer is the right architecture for type-narrowing rejections. MCP-only rejection defends only the MCP surface; storage-layer rejection defends every caller uniformly. Pattern reusable for any future type drop.
  - Forensic discovery tags on auto-migrations preserve user trust. The `v6-migrated:from-decision` tag means users can recover their pre-v6 decision-typed lore entries via tag filter even after the type itself is gone. Silent migration would have lost this affordance.
  - Same-day v5.39 → v6.0 cut works when the bridge is genuinely additive. v5.39.0 (additive framework) shipped 12:37; v6.0.0 (breaking final) shipped 16:48 same day. Bridge-then-finalize sequencing keeps each commit narrow and provides a calendar marker for downstream consumers to synchronize against.
  - 'Test harness as regression infrastructure beats test harness as one-shot gate. PLSAT harness''s graceful-skip mode means it stays useful indefinitely — every fresh adopter benefits from the structural validation, and the source-equivalence checks gracefully degrade post-cleanup. Pattern: design test infra to outlive the migration it validates.'
